On Sat, Sep 16, 2006 at 11:45:20AM -0700, John H. wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
> > I think there are already enough possibilities to spend ones gold.
> > You can buy stuff in shops, ask the Oracle for direction or donate to
> > priests.

I've ascended about half a dozen characters, and I've NEVER had to use
the Oracle's services. I find the advice pretty useless, and the
experience gain not worthwhile since you can gain it yourself pretty
quickly anyway. Donating to priests only goes so far: once you reach AC
1 or AC 0 (intrinsic), priests too become useless. Once you loot Fort
Ludios, there's just about nothing left to do with the money: unless
you're extremely over-experienced for Ludios with no protection from
priests, there's no way you're gonna spend all that money on priests.


> I disagree -- there's far too much gold in the game relative its uses.
> By the end, players almost always have tens of thousands of gold
> pieces lying around without purpose, which either gets toted up to the
> high altar for what amounts to an extremely minor score bonus, or left
> behind in the dungeon.

Precisely. Coins are heavy, and worth so little, that most of my
ascensions have just left them in the dungeon. Why bother with coins if
you can instead carry gems, which are worth a lot more and weigh far
less? Being burdened is a bad way to enter the Planes.


> Part of money's uselessness is Nethack's philosophy that any NPC can
> be attacked and killed.  What's to stop a player from killing someone
> cash has been given to and getting it back?

Here's an idea: how about having the NPC spirit away the money somewhere
the player can't reach every once in a while? I can't imagine a real
shopkeeper walking about carrying millions of coins in his pocket, even
if you allow for the fantasy element of Nethack. It would be more
realistic if, every X turns (or every time the player leaves the leve),
the shopkeeper would deposit part of his cash somewhere safe (i.e., it's
gone from the game).

Of course, the NPC will likely retrieve some of his deposit if he got
into debt, but still, some of the deposit could be gone forever if we
assume they're to pay his living expenses (and any other expenses).


> If a lawful character does this to a Shopkeeper, it's not even a huge
> alignment penalty.

I think this is a design flaw. At *least*, lawful characters should get
a heavy penalty for doing this. As it stands right now, lawful NH
characters are pretty lawless. :-)


> Doing this with a priest will at least cause the player to lose all
> protection, which at least shows the devteam is looking at the
> implications of NPC murder in the game's for-pay services.

However, this is (too) easily circumvented by having a pet do the dirty
work.


[...]
> What I would do is:
> 1. Severely increase the player's score bonus for gold, maybe by a
> factor of ten.  Considerations would have to be made for characters
> who start with extra gold, and maybe it should only be a x5 bonus if
> the player doesn't ascend.
> 2. Make shopkeepers tougher.  Even if they don't provide services.

I think shopkeepers should be fixed so that they don't only react upon
every hit by a pet. What sane person would stand there and be bitten to
death, only returning blows if they come his way first? I think after
the first hit from a pet, the shopkeeper should go berserk and hunt down
the critter and kill it off (or at least chase it out of the shop),
regardless of whether the pet attacks him again.


> 3. Find some non-economic uses for gold.  Gold is rustproof, maybe a
> large quantity of gold pieces can be used to apply plating to a
> weapon?  What can real-life gold be used for that could be brought
> into the game to serve as an unrecoverable sink for lots of gold
> pieces?

What advantage would a gold-plated weapon have over a non-plated one?


> 4. Find services that can be sold to the player that results in gold
> being unrecoverable.  Maybe vending machines?  Maybe a monster NPC
> that eats gold given to him?
[...]

NPC's that spend their money. Not everyone in the world is a miser that
hogs every last penny they earn.

In fact, richer NPC's should be liable to spend *more* money; this way,
the priest who gets, say, 10000zm protection money would be likely to
spend, say, half of that amount to upgrade his temple (like gold-plate
the altar, decorate the temple, or some such), whereas a priest who only
has 100zm will be more stingy. This way, money gets drained from the
game faster if the player has been amassing lots of it.


